Pillar 2: Inhabitants
Persistent NPC 1: Jarl Skald the Elder
Sourced: living-world.md, The Pale section, verbatim — "a Stormcloak
sympathizer so committed to the cause that he has become politically
useless, seeing Imperial agents in every shadow."
Core Motivation: To protect his hold from an Empire he's convinced is
actively working against it. He cannot separate this conviction from the
actual crisis in front of him.
Current State: His response to the nightmares and the frozen people on
his roads is to blame Imperial sabotage (living-world.md, verbatim: "his
response is to blame Imperial sabotage"). He is not lying to the party —
he genuinely believes it.
Potential Evolution: Nothing the party does changes his framework easily.
Direct evidence of Erandur's work might shift him toward taking the
nightmares seriously as a real (non-Imperial) threat; nothing shifts his
read on the frozen people, since Ismara is a shape he has no category
for at all.

Persistent NPC 2: Erandur
Sourced: living-world.md, The Pale section, verbatim — "A priest of Mara
named Erandur — who was once Vaermina's priest and is trying to undo what
he did — has arrived in Dawnstar and is working quietly to destroy the
artifact."
Core Motivation: To destroy the Skull of Corruption and end what he
started. Genuine, private guilt, not performed.
Current State: Working alone, quietly, without Skald's knowledge or
help.
Potential Evolution: Sourced — "Erandur eventually destroys the Skull of
Corruption with or without the party's help — he has been working toward
this for months and doesn't need them." If the party helps: faster
resolution, possibly a combat encounter at the Skull's cave (unbuilt —
GM's call whether this becomes Adventure 2's optional fight instead of
the Morthal coven, since only one is likely to happen at a real table).
If ignored: resolves off-screen; "the nightmares in Dawnstar stop. The
frozen people on the road remain."

Pillar 3: Events
Roll on this table for a full day of downtime in Dawnstar, or texture
entering a new part of town.
d10 | Event
1   | Sleepless Crowd: A knot of townsfolk outside the Windpeak Inn,
    | hollow-eyed, comparing the same dream — something vast, cold,
    | watching. None of them have connected it to the figures on the
    | road. Neither should the party, necessarily — that's the point.
2   | Skald's Watch: Skald has doubled the gate watch against
    | "Imperial saboteurs." The guards are exhausted and jumpy, not
    | actually looking for anything real.
3   | The Mine Slows: Iron-Breaker Mine's foreman reports workers too
    | tired to make quota. Nobody's connected this to the Skull either.
4   | A Standing Figure, Closer Than Expected: One of Ismara's frozen
    | people is visible from the town wall — the first one anyone in
    | Dawnstar has seen this close. Reactions are panic, not
    | recognition; nobody here has the Windhelm context the party has.
5   | Erandur, Glimpsed: A robed man moves toward Nightcaller Temple
    | alone, at an hour nobody else is awake for. If followed (Guile
    | Standard to track him without being noticed), the party can find
    | him before he'd choose to introduce himself.
6   | Thoring's Complaint: The innkeeper is out of sleeping draughts
    | again — third time this week. Good opening to ask what's
    | actually keeping the town up.
7   | A Nightmare, Described: Any NPC willing to talk describes the
    | same dream, unprompted, in slightly different words each time —
    | frozen in place, something vast overhead, being watched. [GATED
    | CONTENT NOTE: this is real, usable information — the dream
    | content genuinely describes Ismara's phenomenology, per living-
    | world.md, even though its actual cause is Vaermina amplifying
    | ordinary anxiety. Don't withhold this from a player who asks; the
    | confusion is the design, not a lock without a key behind it.]
8   | Quiet Day: Nothing unusual, just cold and tired. Order-of-business
    | scene material.
9   | Skald's Accusation: Skald corners the party (or a visibly
    | Imperial-coded member of it) about "Legion business" in his hold.
    | Suspicious, not violent — he wants them gone, or wants answers he
    | won't get.
10  | The Skull's Reach: Someone in town has a violent episode —
    | screaming awake, lashing out — clearly worse than ordinary bad
    | sleep. This is the spore effect intensifying; a concrete beat if
    | the party needs a reason to take the "just nightmares" thread
    | seriously.
